 {
  "key": "polynomials.remainder-factor.q14",
  "type": "EXTENDED_RESPONSE",
  "concepts": [
   "amath.polynomials.remainder-factor"
  ],
  "difficulty": 3,
  "marks": 3,
  "estimatedSeconds": 270,
  "stem": "Prove the remainder theorem: when a polynomial $f(x)$ is divided by $x - a$, the remainder is $f(a)$.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "M1 writes $f(x) \\equiv (x - a)q(x) + r$ with $r$ a constant (remainder of lower degree than $x - a$)",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "M1 substitutes $x = a$, making the factor $(x - a)$ zero",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p3",
      "description": "A1 concludes $f(a) = r$",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "modelAnswer": "Dividing by the linear expression $x - a$ gives a quotient $q(x)$ and a constant remainder $r$:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\equiv (x - a)\\,q(x) + r\n$$\n\nThis is an identity, true for all $x$. Put $x = a$:\n\n$$\nf(a) = (a - a)\\,q(a) + r = 0 + r = r\n$$\n\nSo the remainder is $f(a)$."
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "The division identity holds for every x, so choosing x = a kills the term containing the quotient and leaves only the remainder.",
  "hints": [
   "Start from dividend = divisor × quotient + remainder."
  ],
  "skill": "UNDERSTANDING",
  "calculator": false
 },
